{"id":788,"date":"2020-11-05T11:06:40","date_gmt":"2020-11-05T19:06:40","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=788"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:35","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:35","slug":"lists","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/lists\/","title":{"rendered":"3B: Lists"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167429681\" name=\"6a22167429681\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>What are lists?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In Python, lists store and organize data of the same type without you having to create multiple variables. For example, this list could represent your classes:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">subjects = ['algebra', 'english',\u00a0 'computer science', 'history', 'biology']<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Important: these lists are <\/span><b>ordered<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. This means that each item has a position in the list called the <\/span><b>index<\/b><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, which is an integer that refers to its position in the list <\/span><i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">starting from 0<\/span><\/i><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. The first element in the list is at index 0 because it is 0 steps away from the beginning of the list. In any case that involves counting, you will be starting from 0, so don&#8217;t forget!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Having an index is like having the address of where your information lives. A particular value can be accessed by the name of the list and its <\/span><b>index:<\/b><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">listName[index]<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">So if you wanted to access your class list above, it would look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">subjects[index]<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221674297bb\" name=\"6a221674297bb\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Play a note<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To explain lists, we\u2019re going to be using the buzzer on your CoDrone Mini\u2019s remote! Since musical notes are part of groups called scales, you will be able to hear your lists in action.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The first thing you need to do is play one note. Import the CoDrone Mini and note libraries, create the drone object, and pair to your CoDrone Mini. Next, play the note! The first parameter is the note itself, and the second parameter is how many seconds it will play for. We used middle C for our program, but you can use whatever note you like.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try this out and see what the note sounds like!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Note\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.play_note(Note.C4, 0.5)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167429834\" name=\"6a22167429834\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Play a note with frequency<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Unless you know music really well, there\u2019s a good chance you\u2019re not sure the names of the notes you will need to use. Instead, you can use frequency! In music, and sound in general, frequency is the number of pulses or vibrations per second, and it\u2019s measured in hertz. The higher the frequency, the higher the pitch, or sound, is.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To try this out, use the program that you made in the last step. However, replace \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">Note.C4<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">with \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">261<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, and then try it out. Did you get the same thing?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Note\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.play_note(261, 0.5) #261 Hz is Note.C4<\/pre>\n<p>Use a frequency table <a href=\"https:\/\/pages.mtu.edu\/~suits\/notefreqs.html\">like this one<\/a> to find the frequencies of more notes, and then experiment with them in your program! Just as a warning, decimal points will not work in the frequency parameter, so you will need to round to the closest whole number.[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221674298a6\" name=\"6a221674298a6\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Make your own music<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Compose your own song using notes or frequencies! To give your song some rhythm, use the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">time.sleep()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function between notes or groups of notes. Try our program out and then write yours!<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Note\r\n\r\nImport time\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.play_note(Note.C4, 0.5)\r\n\r\ndrone.play_note(Note.D4, 0.5)\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(0.25)\r\n\r\ndrone.play_note(Note.E4, 0.5)\r\n\r\ndrone.play_note(Note.F4, 0.5)\r\n\r\ntime.sleep(0.5)\r\n\r\ndrone.play_note(Note.G4, 0.5)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167429932\" name=\"6a22167429932\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Make a list<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you\u2019ve made some music with the remote, let\u2019s get back to lists.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">In music, scales are groups of eight notes that increase or decrease in pitch, and they typically start with a C note. What you will be doing in this activity is making a list of notes for a C-major scale and then using that list to play specific notes.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the CoDrone Mini and note libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing to CoDrone Mini, create a list called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">notes<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. Next, add in the notes for your scale. Your scale can start with C3, C4, C5, C6, or C7, and then go all the way up or down to the next octave, or the note that\u2019s 8 steps away from the first note.<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">notes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once the list is finished, you can practice playing a specific note from the list. This is what that line of code will look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">drone.play_note(notes[0], 0.5) # change the index to change notes<\/pre>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Remember that counting starts at 0, so \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">notes[0]<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">will play the first note, \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">notes[1]<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">will play the second, and so on.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is what your program should look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Note\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nnotes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.play_note(notes[0], 0.5) # change the index to change notes<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221674299f8\" name=\"6a221674299f8\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 6<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Get the length of your list<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Lists can be long, and there may be times when you forget how many items your list has! To help with this, you can use the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">len()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function. It will print out the number of items in your list.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Try it out by using <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">print(len(notes))<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">after your list in your last program. Does your list have the correct amount of items?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Note\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nnotes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]\r\n\r\n\r\nprint(len(notes))<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167429a6e\" name=\"6a22167429a6e\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 7<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Lists and loops<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You can use a loop to play every note in your list one time for 0.5 seconds. To do this, use your code from the last step, but take out the print command.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use a for loop that will access the length of the notes list and then play them for 0.5 seconds each. Since the range of the for loop is the length of the list, each note will play only one time.\u00a0<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">This is what your program should look like:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Note\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nnotes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(len(notes)):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.play_note(notes[i], 0.5)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167429ae4\" name=\"6a22167429ae4\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 8<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Additional lists<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Just like variables, you can have more than one list in your program! These are great to have if you have different data types or want to keep your items categorized. For this step, you will be adding another list for note duration.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Use your program from the last step and then create a list called \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">duration<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. This list should be the same size as the notes list, so include eight note durations. They can all be different, or some can be the same. Just remember that these durations are in seconds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, add a for loop to play both the notes and their durations, and then run your program to see what it sounds like! You can always add or subtract items from their lists. Just make sure you have the same amount of items in both.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your program should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nfrom CoDrone_mini import Note\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\nnotes = [Note.C3, Note.D3, Note.E3, Note.F3, Note.G3, Note.A3, Note.B3, Note.C4]\r\n\r\nduration = [0.1, 0.1, 0.25, 0.25, 0.25, 0.1, 0.1, 0.5]\r\n\r\n\r\nfor i in range(len(notes)):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.play_note(notes[i], duration[i])<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a22167429b34\" name=\"6a22167429b34\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 9<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: Make Some Noise<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Now that you know how to use lists for both notes and durations, see if you can program your drone\u2019s remote to play a popular song! This can be something short or a long classical symphony. If you know how to read sheet music, you can use that, but you can also use trial and error to figure notes out!<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 10 What are lists? [vc_column_text]In Python, lists store and organize data of the same type without you having to create multiple variables. For example, this list could represent&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/lists\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1730,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":744,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1737,1782,1848,1793,1812,1826,1575,1438,1252,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2137,2122,2134],"class_list":["post-788","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-lists","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/788","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":6,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/788\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2866,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/788\/revisions\/2866"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1730"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=788"}],"wp:term":[{"taxonomy":"lesson-category","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-category?post=788"},{"taxonomy":"lesson-tag","embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lesson-tag?post=788"}],"curies":[{"name":"wp","href":"https:\/\/api.w.org\/{rel}","templated":true}]}}